      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High-density racking can indeed create significant vapor accumulation \"dead zones,\" particularly in the deep bays or between uprights where airflow is restricted. These localized areas prevent even well-placed sensor arrays from achieving uniform gas detection, as lighter vapors might stratify higher while heavier ones pool near the floor. For an adjuster, this risk is substantial because it suggests a potential ignition source could encounter a high concentration of flammable vapor without an early warning. Thorough investigation would require supplemental methods like sniffers to detect pockets not covered by static sensors.",
      "source": "target",
